#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""HasH-Arch Settings  (hash-settings)

A standalone, dark/rounded Settings app for HasH-Arch that looks and works like
the Windows 11 Settings app, fully native — it drives the REAL Linux backends
(kwriteconfig6/kreadconfig6, nmcli, bluetoothctl, pactl/wpctl, powerprofilesctl,
systemctl, journalctl, pacman, qdbus …) directly.  There are NO KDE popups and
no embedded KDE config modules — every control drives a real backend itself.

Chrome (frameless rounded #12141a, draggable TitleBar, ACCENT #56c5d8) is a
Windows-11-style shell: a left category rail with an account header on top, a
global search at the very top, and a main pane that is a navigation stack
(NavStack) with a clickable Breadcrumb + Back.  Card rows (Win11Row) are either
drill-ins (chevron → pushes a sub-page) or inline controls (toggle/combo/slider).

A small declarative ENGINE makes settings tractable and scriptable: each setting
is data — a `Setting(key, name, control, backend, apply=…)` — backed by
`KConfig` (kreadconfig6/kwriteconfig6), `Cmd` (argv read/write, pkexec when
privileged) or `Info` (read-only display).  `SchemaPage` renders a list of
Settings into Win11Rows; `apply` hooks (e.g. kwin_reconfigure) run after a write.
This also yields a headless CLI for free:

    hash-settings get <key>       print a setting's current value
    hash-settings set <key> <v>   write a setting (drives the same backend)

Native pages (Network, Firewall, Appearance, Display & Sound, Power, System,
Services, Event Viewer, Users, Updates) and embedded HasH-tool panes (Accounts,
Security, Disks, Apps) all live inside this Win11 shell.

Entry-list `kind` is informational for the search index only:
    page  -> a native in-app settings page
    hash  -> a pane embedding a HasH tool's backend

Usage:
    hash-settings                 launch the GUI (Overview landing)
    hash-settings --page network  open straight on a category
    hash-settings get <key>       print a setting value (headless)
    hash-settings set <key> <v>   write a setting (headless)
    hash-settings --shot <png>    (internal) grab a screenshot then exit
"""
